A||||9||MooCow||Do I have to drop the cradle??||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||11-20-2001||12:51 AM||||I have went through the searches and couldn't find any answers at all.  As some of you may know my tranny has a nice 8 inch crack in it.  I already have a replacement tranny.  Can I just unbolt the other tranny and then re bolt this one back on??  I know its not that easy, but can I?? The car is already on jack stands next to my house till either I fix it, sell it, or just wait and do whatever with it.  I am kinda bumbed cause I am still in debt $3,000 with a Fiero that I only got to drive 6,000 miles on before the tranny cracked.  Well let me know if I can or not.<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forum_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||11-20-2001||12:57 AM||||Well.......... you could drop the <I>rear</I> of the cradle and fanagle it out -- kinda hard to tell ya because I always remove the engine when I replace a tranny but I am in a shop with a lift and hoist and it is much easier -- I suppose you could drop the rear though and get it off that way....... it's going to mean jacking up the engine though so that the tranny clears the cradle and is free of the mounts - then you will have to remove the wheel and strut on the left side to remove it (try not to loosen the 24mm bolts where the strut mounts to the knuckle and you won't have to have an alignment when you put it all back together.)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||MooCow||11-20-2001||01:13 AM||||So do you think it would be safe for me to do it that way??  Yea the car is up on jack stands and I can get under there very easy, but will it move when I start to jack the engine up??  I dont have hoist or anything just your basic tools.  But I am able to get to all the bolts I need to do it then if I drop the rear of the craddle??  How long do you think it would take the average person to do it this way?? <P>Thanks Ken||||reg||10
Z||000003||dennis_6||11-20-2001||01:35 AM||||You can rent engine hoist fairly cheap.  I would look into that first.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Brandon86SE||11-20-2001||02:34 AM||||My friend I go to school with up here used to own a mechanics shop in Buffalo, and a few weeks ago I thought I might have a shot clutch.  He's worked on pleny of Fiero's, so I asked him about it, whether the engine HAD to come out or not.  He said no, if you get it up on stands, or a lift, you can remove just a couple of bolts, and be able to get at both the Tranny and Clutch.  The reason this isn't done more often is that it is easier if you have the equipment to pull the engine, plus it's also more time consuming, so mechanics typically do it to charge more.  But he said that with access to a lift we could change my clutch in a day on a weekend if need be.  So, knowing this, I'd say go for it!<P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000005||Black86SE||11-20-2001||02:48 AM||||When Joe and I removed my engine we rented a lift and it cost me $35. I got it for two days too cause they wern't open on sunday. That is what most of the shops I called were charging.||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||11-20-2001||04:58 AM||||Ken, if you do it that way just don't jack the engine up higher than the jack stands!! -- you only have to raise the engine about six inches off of the cradle to clear everything.....||||reg||1
Z||000007||thomas_l||11-20-2001||05:05 AM||||Cliff notes version of what's on Ed Parks site and it worked for me:<P>1. Remove the trans mounts<BR>2. Put a 2 X 4 under the starter and let the engine sit on it.<BR>3. Remove the LH strut assembly.<BR>4. Clear all the other stuff (halfshafts, clutch slave, etc)<BR>5. Put a jack under the cradle. Remove the back two bolts and the front left. Lower the jack watching the coolant hoses.<BR>6. Trans goes out the left wheel well.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||11-20-2001||01:09 PM||||FWIW, I R&R'ed the (4-speed Muncie) transaxle on my 1986 SE V6 Fiero using the Ed Parks "rock the cradle" method described in his website.  The top of the left rear wheel well opening was only 34" off my garage floor, if that helps.      ||||reg||1
Z||000009||MooCow||11-20-2001||01:19 PM||||Well I asked Ed Parks and he said look on his web site and I started following everything, but I am giving up.  I can't even get the damn calliper off!!  So I am just going to wait till I either have help from someone that knows what they are doing or wait till it gets warmer come srping.  I am sure I can do it myself but just don't want to anymore.  My car just seems like a $3000 piece of junk.  So thanks for all the help and I will keep them in mind if and when I ever decide to do it.<P>Thanks Ken||||reg||1
